WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.715
Threat hunting is a proactive cybersecurity activity in which analysts search for evidence

00:00:04.715 --> 00:00:05.820
of malicious activity,

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.985
suspicious behavior, or hidden threats that may have evaded automated detection systems.

00:00:10.985 --> 00:00:13.120
Traditional security operations often begin with an alert.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.039
A tool identifies activity that matches a rule or analytic, and an analyst responds.

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:20.960
Hunting begins with a question and actively looks for evidence,

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.250
even when no alert has fired.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.055
The purpose is not to distrust every security tool.

00:00:25.055 --> 00:00:29.280
Automated detection remains essential because it observes large environments continuously.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.850
Hunting complements that coverage by exploring uncertainty, testing assumptions,

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.660
and examining behavior that may not fit known alert logic.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.235
A hunt may focus on a business concern, a change in threat intelligence,

00:00:39.235 --> 00:00:43.200
an unusual pattern, a high-value service, or a suspected visibility gap.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.000
The investigation should have a defined scope and a clear defensive purpose.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.215
Hunters combine analytical judgment with telemetry from endpoints, identities, networks,

00:00:51.215 --> 00:00:53.040
applications, and cloud services.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.360
They compare observations with expected behavior and follow evidence carefully rather than

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.860
relying on intuition alone.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.285
Not every hunt discovers a threat.

00:01:00.285 --> 00:01:03.950
A well-run hunt can still improve asset knowledge, expose missing logs,

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.270
clarify normal activity, refine detections,

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.280
and help security teams understand where their controls are strong or weak.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.400
Threat hunting is part of a larger defensive lifecycle.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.000
Findings may inform monitoring, detection engineering, incident response,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.180
vulnerability management, identity security, architecture, and risk decisions.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.800
This course provides educational information about threat-hunting concepts.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.630
It is not operational incident-response advice, offensive security instruction,

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.120
or guidance for conducting unauthorized activity.
